# Normalise line endings on commit.
#
# Why this file exists: the tree was materialised on a Windows volume without
# any line-ending rule, so every text file was checked out with CRLF while the
# git index holds LF. The result was that `git status` reported all ~64 tracked
# files as modified in a clean checkout, which hides real edits, makes any
# content hash depend on the platform, and would have pushed a full-tree CRLF
# commit the first time someone staged changes from Windows.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text files and store LF in the repository
# regardless of how they were checked out; `eol=lf` keeps LF on checkout too, so
# a checkout is byte-identical everywhere. Binary assets are left untouched by
# automatic detection; the explicit patterns below cover the types git would
# otherwise be tempted to normalise inside a recovery tree.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never convert these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.webp binary
*.gif  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.z    binary
*.gz   binary
*.lz4  binary
*.img  binary
*.so   binary
*.ko   binary
*.apk  binary
*.jar  binary
*.zip  binary

# Patches and hex dumps must keep their exact bytes.
*.patch text eol=lf
*.diff  text eol=lf
